# Auto-generated plain-language "how this is calculated" text, one entry per page.
# Faithful to the model code (pipeline/model.py, config/*). Regenerate if the model changes.

CALC = {
    'Overview': "**How this headline is calculated**\n\nEvery metro's reusable-lumber number is built by running one transparent supply cascade on each demolition, then summing. We start from demolition permits, estimate the wood-frame floor area torn down, convert that to framing lumber, then step down through what can realistically be recovered, cleaned, and regraded to spec-ready stock. Carbon is computed from the spec-ready total. Every coefficient is sourced on the Assumptions page and can be overridden by a scenario or slider.\n\nPer demolition row (one per metro, building-era cohort, and structure type):\n\n- **Floor area:** permits times floor_area_m2 times wood_frame_likelihood (how likely the building is light wood-frame).\n- **Framing lumber:** floor_area_m2 times framing_bf_per_m2 (dimensional structural lumber per square metre; this already is the framing, measured directly by McKeever and Phelps).\n- **Gross wood content (headline 1):** framing_bf divided by 0.78 (dimensional_share_of_total). This grosses framing up to total wood (framing plus panels plus finish). It is a headline only and does NOT feed the recovery steps below.\n- **Condition factor:** max(0.20, 0.85 minus 0.020 times decades), where decades is the cohort's representative age (pre-1946 = 9.0 down to post-2010 = 1.0). The 0.20 is a hard floor.\n- **Recoverable (headline 2):** framing_bf times 0.30 (recovery_method_factor, blended mixed practice) times condition times (1 minus contamination_discount for that era). Contamination runs 0.08 for pre-1946 stock up to 0.45 for post-2000 stock, because newer buildings use more engineered wood that cannot be reclaimed as dimensional lumber.\n- **Salvageable dimensional:** recoverable times 0.70 (denail_sort_yield, what survives denailing, sorting, trimming).\n- **Spec-ready reusable (headline 3):** salvageable times 0.55 (grading_pass_rate, the share passing a No.2-or-better structural regrade).\n- **Reclaimed value (headline 4):** spec_ready_bf times the CAD-per-board-foot for the building's value tier (low 5.00, medium 6.50, high 9.00 CAD/bf; only pre-war homes get the high old-growth tier).\n\nRolling up across metros:\n\n- Each of the four headline numbers is a sum: first across the five eras and four structure types inside a metro, then across the selected metros. With the province filter left at its default (all of Canada), it sums all 25 CMAs.\n- **The province multiselect changes the four metrics** (it sums only the selected provinces' metros). The carbon sentence below does NOT change with the filter: it always uses the national spec-ready total.\n\nCarbon (from the national spec-ready total, in board feet):\n\n- **Avoided new-lumber manufacturing:** spec_ready times 0.0023597 (m3 per board foot) times 62.0 kg CO2e/m3, divided by 1000 to get tonnes.\n- **Biogenic carbon kept in use:** spec_ready times 0.0023597 times 785.0 kg CO2e/m3, divided by 1000.\n\nConstants used (defaults; sources on the Assumptions page):\n\n- recovery_method_factor 0.30 (Oregon DEQ 2019; Delta Institute)\n- condition_base 0.85, degradation_per_decade 0.020/decade (engineering placeholder; Cavalli et al. 2016) (estimated)\n- denail_sort_yield 0.70 (Falk FPL-RP-650, 2008)\n- grading_pass_rate 0.55 (Falk FPL-RP-650; Arbelaez et al. 2019)\n- dimensional_share_of_total 0.78, gross headline only (McKeever 1994 volume math; Oregon DEQ 2019)\n- contamination_discount by era: 0.08 / 0.22 / 0.35 / 0.45 / 0.45 (synthesis of APA/SBCA engineered-wood shares plus Oregon DEQ) (estimated)\n- framing_bf_per_m2 by archetype: 85 pre-war, 79 post-war, 80 modern, 70 recent, 47.9 low-rise multi, 55.8 semi and row (Falk FPL 2013; McKeever and Phelps FPL 1994; Elling and McKeever APA 2015; recent value reasoned)\n- floor_area_m2 by archetype: 115 / 120 / 175 / 205 / 100 / 123 (MPAC Ontario 2024; NRCan SHEU 2015; Elling and McKeever 2015)\n- wood_frame_likelihood: 0.98 / 0.98 / 0.97 / 0.95 / 0.70 / 0.96 / 0.95 (NRCan, NBC Part 9, CMHC, CWC)\n- value_per_bf_cad: low 5.00, medium 6.50, high 9.00 CAD/bf (reclaimed dealer price lists 2023-2026, USD-CAD 1.37)\n- demolition_type_share: detached 0.82, semi 0.012, row 0.014, apartment 0.154 (StatCan 34-10-0285-01, 2022)\n- teardown_propensity by era: 2.5 / 1.6 / 1.0 / 0.5 / 0.2 (reasoned age-hazard curve) (estimated)\n- carbon: 0.0023597 m3/bf, 62.0 kg CO2e/m3 avoided manufacturing (2024 review; Athena 2018), 785.0 kg CO2e/m3 biogenic (Athena 2018)",
    'Municipal baseline': "This page turns each city's demolition activity into an annual estimate of reusable framing lumber and its resale value. It does not run its own math: it reads the shared supply cascade (model.estimate_supply on the demolition table from ingest.build_demolition_table), filters to the CMA you pick, and sums. Every coefficient is sourced in config/assumptions.py and can be overridden with the sliders. Here is the exact chain, in order.\n\n**How the demolition flow is built (steps 1 to 3):**\n- **Annual demolition permits:** most CMAs use dwellings times demolition_intensity (a sourced per-dwelling rate). Toronto uses a live permit feed and Vancouver a sourced count.\n- **Era mix of demolitions:** older homes are torn down faster, so each cohort's standing-stock share (StatCan vintage) is weighted by a teardown_propensity factor and renormalised: emix = (vintage times propensity) divided by the sum across cohorts. This is an estimate, not a measured series.\n- **Structure-type split:** each era is split by structure type into its own row: permits = annual times demolition_type_share times emix. Detached rows take the era's archetype; semi, row and apartment take their own archetype.\n\n**The recovery cascade per row (steps 4 to 11):**\n- **Floor area:** floor_area_m2 = permits times floor_area_m2 (per archetype) times wood_frame_likelihood.\n- **Framing lumber:** framing_bf = floor_area_m2 times framing_bf_per_m2. This is the dimensional structural lumber the model tracks. Total wood content, shown elsewhere, is framing divided by dimensional_share_of_total (0.78).\n- **Age condition:** condition = max(0.20, 0.85 minus 0.020 times decades), where decades is the cohort's age in decades before the base year (pre1946 = 9.0 down to post2010 = 1.0).\n- **Recoverable:** recoverable_bf = framing_bf times recovery_method_factor (0.30) times condition times (1 minus contamination_discount for that era). The contamination discount rises for newer stock, which carries more engineered wood.\n- **Salvageable dimensional:** salvageable_bf = recoverable_bf times denail_sort_yield (0.70).\n- **Spec-ready reusable:** spec_ready_bf = salvageable_bf times grading_pass_rate (0.55). This is the narrowest, highest-confidence category.\n- **Reclaimed value:** value_cad = spec_ready_bf times value_per_bf_cad for the archetype's value tier (low 5.00, medium 6.50, high 9.00 CAD per board foot).\n\n**The four headline metrics, the funnel, and the archetype bar** are just these columns summed across the selected CMA's cohort-by-type rows. The Dwellings figure is displayed straight from StatCan 2021, not computed.\n\n**Constants used (default point values; each also carries a low/high range for the uncertainty model):**\n- recovery_method_factor = 0.30 fraction, Oregon DEQ 2019 / Delta Institute (blended mixed practice).\n- condition_base = 0.85 fraction, engineering placeholder plus Oregon DEQ 2019 (estimate).\n- degradation_per_decade = 0.020 fraction/decade, Cavalli et al. 2016.\n- denail_sort_yield = 0.70 fraction, Falk FPL-RP-650 2008 (inferred).\n- grading_pass_rate = 0.55 fraction, Falk FPL-RP-650 / Arbelaez et al. 2019.\n- contamination_discount by era = 0.08 / 0.22 / 0.35 / 0.45 / 0.45 (pre1946 to post2010), synthesis of Oregon DEQ plus engineered-wood-share literature.\n- dimensional_share_of_total = 0.78 fraction, McKeever 1994 / Oregon DEQ 2019 (used only for the total-wood headline).\n- teardown_propensity by era = 2.5 / 1.6 / 1.0 / 0.5 / 0.2, estimated age-hazard curve (no Canadian vintage-of-demolitions data exists).\n- demolition_type_share = detached 0.82, semi 0.012, row 0.014, apartment 0.154, StatCan Building Permits 34-10-0285-01, Canada 2022.\n- value_per_bf_cad = low 5.00, medium 6.50, high 9.00 CAD/bf, reclaimed dealer price lists 2023 to 2026, USD-CAD about 1.37.\n- Per-archetype physical inputs (framing_bf_per_m2, floor_area_m2, wood_frame_likelihood, value_tier): Falk FPL 2013 + McKeever 1994, Elling & McKeever 2015 (APA), MPAC Ontario 2024, NRCan SHEU 2015, NRCan / NBC Part 9, CMHC. For example sfd_prewar uses 85 bf/m2, 115 m2, 0.98 likelihood, high value tier.\n\nNote: teardown_propensity, condition_base, denail_sort_yield and the contamination_discount series are reasoned or inferred estimates rather than directly measured figures; everything else is a cited source. All values are the defaults and can be changed live with the sliders (base_year and dimensional_share_of_total are the only non-editable ones).",
    'Hotspots & archetypes': 'This page is the Toronto worked example for demolition hotspots. It maps where teardowns cluster and tags each cluster with its dominant building archetype, then explains why some archetypes are worth targeting. Two honest caveats up front: the neighbourhood permit counts on the map are illustrative of the method, and the city total plus archetype mix are what stay anchored to real Toronto Open Data.\n\n**What the page actually computes**\n- **Marker size:** annual_permits, the raw illustrative permit count for each neighbourhood. No math.\n- **Marker color and table label:** a lookup, reg["archetypes"][dominant_archetype]["label"]. No math.\n\n**Why one archetype beats another (the ranking claim in the info box)**\nEach archetype is run through the same supply cascade (in estimate_supply). For a given number of permits:\n- **Wood-frame floor area:** permits times floor_area_m2 times wood_frame_likelihood.\n- **Framing lumber (bf):** floor area times framing_bf_per_m2.\n- **Age condition:** max(0.20, condition_base minus degradation_per_decade times the cohort\'s representative decades).\n- **Recoverable (bf):** framing times recovery_method_factor (0.30) times condition times (1 minus the era\'s contamination_discount).\n- **Salvageable dimensional (bf):** recoverable times denail_sort_yield (0.70).\n- **Spec-ready reusable (bf):** salvageable times grading_pass_rate (0.55).\n- **Reclaimed value (CAD):** spec-ready times value_per_bf_cad for the archetype\'s value_tier.\n- **Gross total wood (headline only):** framing divided by dimensional_share_of_total (0.78). This is not part of the reusable cascade.\n- **Ranking:** cma_summary sums each column by city and sorts by spec_ready_bf, and the same per-archetype constants are what make post-war win on volume and pre-war win on price.\n\nSo the ranking is driven by four archetype-specific levers: how much lumber per m2 (framing_bf_per_m2), how much building (floor_area_m2 times wood_frame_likelihood), how much survives (the era contamination_discount), and price per bf (value_tier). Post-war (1946-1980) pairs a high wood-frame likelihood, high framing intensity, and low contamination loss, so it carries the most reusable lumber. Pre-1946 has the highest framing intensity, the lowest contamination loss, and sits in the high value tier, so it carries the highest value per teardown even though there are fewer of them. Modern and low-rise multi have lower framing intensity and higher contamination loss, so they yield less.\n\n**Constants used (value, then source)**\n- recovery_method_factor = 0.30 (blended mixed practice), Oregon DEQ 2019 and Delta Institute.\n- condition_base = 0.85, degradation_per_decade = 0.020 per decade. condition_base is an engineering placeholder (estimate); degradation is from Cavalli et al. 2016 and is deliberately weak.\n- denail_sort_yield = 0.70, inferred from Falk FPL-RP-650 2008 (estimate).\n- grading_pass_rate = 0.55, Falk FPL-RP-650 and Arbelaez et al. 2019.\n- contamination_discount by era: pre-1946 0.08, 1946-1980 0.22, 1981-2000 0.35, 2001 and later 0.45. Pre-war is from Oregon DEQ; the middle and newer eras are a synthesis of engineered-wood share (APA/SBCA I-joist data), so they are partly estimated.\n- dimensional_share_of_total = 0.78 (framing to gross headline), McKeever 1994 and Oregon DEQ 2019.\n- value_per_bf_cad: low $5.00, medium $6.50, high $9.00, from reclaimed dealer price lists 2023-2026 at USD-CAD 1.37.\n- Archetype physical constants (framing_bf_per_m2, floor_area_m2, wood_frame_likelihood, value_tier):\n  - pre-1946: 85 bf/m2, 115 m2, 0.98, high tier (Falk FPL 2013 plus McKeever 1994; MPAC Ontario 2024; NRCan and NBC Part 9).\n  - 1946-1980: 79 bf/m2, 120 m2, 0.98, medium tier (McKeever & Phelps FPL 1994; MPAC 2024).\n  - 1981-2000: 80 bf/m2, 175 m2, 0.97, medium tier (McKeever & Phelps FPL 1994; MPAC 2024).\n- cohort representative age (feeds condition): pre-1946 9.0 decades, 1946-1980 5.5, 1981-2000 3.5, 2001-2010 2.0, post-2010 1.0 (representative estimate, model.py).\n\nEvery coefficient above is editable on the Assumptions page, and the ranking follows the numbers you set.',
    'Forecast & uncertainty': "This page runs two engines. A simple compounding forecast projects each city's base-year supply out to 2036, and a 4,000-draw Monte Carlo propagates the recovery-coefficient and data-coverage uncertainty to give P10/P50/P90.\n\n**Multi-year forecast (the lines and shaded band)**\n- **Compound each year:** for a year offset h from 0 to the horizon, factor = (1 + growth) raised to h, and year = base_year + h. Growth defaults to 2.0%/yr and the horizon is 10 years, so the last year is 2036.\n- **Central line:** central = base-year value times factor, computed for every metric (gross, recoverable, salvageable, spec-ready, value).\n- **Coverage band:** low = central times (1 minus band) and high = central times (1 plus band), where band is the city's data-coverage half-width (high 0.10, medium 0.25, low 0.45). Note: the chart calls this the P10-P90 band, but it is the coverage-tier band, not a percentile.\n- **Cumulative table:** sums central, low, and high across all forecast years for each city.\n\n**Monte Carlo (the P10/P50/P90 numbers, base year only)**\n- **4,000 draws, seed 42.** Six coefficients are sampled from triangular(low, point, high) and shared across every city in each draw, so this national error does not diversify away: recovery method factor, denail/sort yield, grading pass rate, condition base, degradation per decade, and a framing-content multiplier proxied from the post-war archetype range (75 divided by 79, 1.0, 86 divided by 79).\n- **Condition:** cond = max(0.20, condition_base minus degradation_per_decade times 5 decades). At the point values that is max(0.20, 0.85 minus 0.10) = 0.75.\n- **Coefficient scaler:** spec_coef = (rmf / 0.30) times (denail / 0.70) times (grading / 0.55) times (cond / 0.75) times framing_mult. The recoverable scaler keeps only (rmf / 0.30) times (cond / 0.75) times framing_mult. Each equals 1.0 at the point values, so it scales the deterministic base-year supply up or down.\n- **Count draw:** each city also gets its own independent triangular(1 minus band, 1, 1 plus band) multiplier on its demolition count, using the same coverage half-widths (high 0.10 / medium 0.25 / low 0.45).\n- **Percentiles:** per city, spec = base spec-ready times spec_coef times count_mult; the national total sums spec across cities. P10, P50, and P90 are the numpy 10th, 50th, and 90th percentiles of the 4,000 draws.\n\n**Constants and sources**\n- **base_year:** 2026 (anchor, fixed).\n- **demolition_growth_rate:** 2.0%/yr, range -1.0% to +5.0%, StatCan Building Permits Survey (default; Toronto can use a live trend).\n- **horizon_years:** 10, range 5 to 15, brief-driven modeling choice.\n- **Coverage band half-widths:** high 0.10 (Toronto Open Data live feed), medium 0.25 (StatCan CMA demolition, 2022), low 0.45 (inferred from housing stock).\n- **recovery_method_factor:** 0.30, range 0.15 to 0.50, Oregon DEQ 2019 / Delta Institute (deconstruction about 0.55, mechanical demolition about 0.05).\n- **condition_base:** 0.85, range 0.70 to 0.95, engineering placeholder plus Oregon DEQ 2019 (estimated, not directly measured).\n- **degradation_per_decade:** 0.020, range 0.000 to 0.050, Cavalli et al. 2016 (weak effect).\n- **denail_sort_yield:** 0.70, range 0.55 to 0.85, Falk FPL-RP-650 2008 (inferred).\n- **grading_pass_rate:** 0.55, range 0.35 to 0.70, Falk FPL-RP-650 2008 / Arbelaez et al. 2019.\n- **Framing-content proxy:** post-war framing 79 bf/m2, range 75 to 86, McKeever & Phelps FPL 1994 (used only to build the framing multiplier).\n- **Monte Carlo:** 4,000 draws, random seed 42, representative age 5 decades, condition floored at 0.20.\n\nEstimated rather than measured: condition_base, denail_sort_yield, degradation_per_decade, the horizon length, and the 2%/yr growth default.",
    'Supply gaps': 'This page does not subtract demand from supply. It sets each metro area\'s spec-ready reusable supply against how many recovery and processing firms operate in its province, then flags markets where supply is high relative to the firms that can handle it. The gap for each market is a ratio (board feet of supply per firm) plus a plain-language flag.\n\n**How each number is built**\n\n- **Firms per province:** province_smes = round(252 times the province\'s share of the national SME census). Shares run from ON 37% and BC 25% down to NL 0.5%, which works out to roughly ON 93, BC 63, QC 40, AB 18, NS 10, MB 8, SK 8, NB 5, PE 5, NL 1 firms.\n- **Attach firms to each metro:** each of the 25 CMAs is mapped to its province, so it inherits that province\'s firm estimate.\n- **Supply per firm:** bf_per_sme = spec-ready board feet divided by the province firm count. If a province has zero mapped firms the ratio is set to infinity and shows as "n/a".\n- **Gap flag (first rule that matches wins, in this order):**\n  - if the province has 8 or fewer firms, flag "Thin ecosystem: few recovery/processing firms in-province";\n  - else if supply per firm is above 60,000 board feet, flag "Under-served: high supply per firm";\n  - else "Workable base".\n- **Ranking and counts:** rows are sorted by spec-ready supply, highest first. "CMAs analyzed" is just the row count (25). "Markets flagged thin or under-served" counts every row whose flag is not "Workable base". The bar chart shows the top 12 CMAs by supply per firm.\n\nThe spec-ready supply figure itself comes from the supply model (the permits-to-spec-ready cascade); this page reuses it as the supply input rather than recomputing it.\n\n**Constants and where they come from**\n\n- **National SME census total: 252 firms.** Light House, "Market Research on SMEs involved in Recovery and Reintegration of CRD Wood," for ECCC, March 2026.\n- **Provincial firm shares (of 252): ON 0.37, BC 0.25, QC 0.16, AB 0.07, NS 0.04, MB 0.03, SK 0.03, NB 0.02, PE 0.02, NL 0.005.** Same Light House report, March 2026.\n- **Thin-ecosystem cutoff: 8 or fewer firms in-province.** This is a chosen classification threshold set in the code, not a sourced figure.\n- **Under-served cutoff: more than 60,000 board feet of supply per firm.** Also a chosen classification threshold in the code, not a sourced figure.\n\nConfidence and limits: the per-province firm counts are the national census scaled by provincial share, so they flag directional pressure, not audited per-firm capacity. The two cutoffs (8 firms and 60,000 board feet per firm) are analyst-set thresholds for labelling, so treat them as judgement calls rather than measured values.',
    'Embodied carbon': "This page takes the total spec-ready reusable lumber the supply model estimates (in board feet), converts it to cubic metres, then to tonnes of CO2e using published emission factors. Three separate climate benefits are calculated and added together.\n\n**Where the input comes from**\n- The starting number is the total spec-ready board feet, summed across every market. It is the final rung of the supply cascade (framing, then recoverable, then salvageable, then spec-ready). This page adds no new supply logic; it only converts that board-feet figure into carbon.\n\n**The steps**\n- **Board feet to cubic metres:** multiply board feet by 0.0023597 m3 per board foot. This is the shared first step for all three benefits. One board foot equals one twelfth of a cubic foot.\n- **Avoided manufacturing:** cubic metres times 62 kg CO2e/m3, divided by 1000 to get tonnes. In one line: spec times 0.0023597 times 62, divided by 1000. This is the new-lumber production you skip by reusing.\n- **Biogenic carbon kept in use:** cubic metres times 785 kg CO2e/m3, divided by 1000. In one line: spec times 0.0023597 times 785, divided by 1000. This is the carbon locked in the wood that stays out of the air while the wood stays in service.\n- **Avoided landfill methane (two parts):** first turn the wood into tonnes: spec times 0.0023597 times 492 (kg per m3), divided by 1000 gives tonnes of wood. Then tonnes of wood times 217 kg CO2e per tonne, divided by 1000 gives tonnes CO2e.\n- **Total climate benefit:** avoided manufacturing plus avoided landfill methane plus biogenic carbon kept in use.\n- **Car equivalent:** divide the total by 4.6, the US EPA figure for tonnes CO2e per passenger vehicle per year.\n- **By market:** the avoided-manufacturing and biogenic formulas are applied to each market's own spec-ready board feet to build the bar chart and the detail table.\n\n**Constants used**\n- **0.0023597 m3 per board foot** (BF_TO_M3): a fixed geometric conversion, one board foot is one twelfth of a cubic foot. Shown rounded to 0.00236 in the caption.\n- **62 kg CO2e per m3** avoided manufacturing: Canadian softwood cradle-to-gate. Athena Institute cradle-to-gate LCA of Canadian softwood lumber (2018) and a 2024 review of Canadian harvested-wood-product emission factors.\n- **785 kg CO2e per m3** biogenic carbon: Athena Institute cradle-to-gate LCA of Canadian softwood lumber (2018).\n- **217 kg CO2e per tonne** of wood diverted from landfill: Napier et al. 2007 via US EPA WARM and Athena, as summarised in USDA Forest Service, Bergman et al. 2013 (FPL-RP-676).\n- **492 kg per m3** wood density (surfaced dry softwood, about 15% moisture), used only to turn board feet into tonnes for the landfill step. An assumed typical density, stated with about.\n- **4.6 t CO2e per vehicle per year** (US EPA), used only for the plain-language car comparison.\n\n**Notes**\n- A displacement cross-check appears in the caption (about 280 kg CO2e per tonne avoided by reuse, UK Forest Research 2025). It is a sanity check only and does not feed any of the numbers above.\n- For context, Toronto Green Standard v4 lets reused or salvaged components count as zero upfront embodied carbon against its 350 (Tier 2) and 250 (Tier 3) kg CO2e/m2 caps. These caps are context, not part of the calculation.",
    'Policy & capacity': 'This simulator models each policy as a lift to the effective recovery rate, the share of structural wood recovered intact, which is the single dominant supply lever. Toggle policies on and it restacks that recovery rate, then rescales spec-ready supply, value and carbon by the same proportion. It does not re-run the whole model; because supply and value are strictly linear in the recovery rate, a simple multiplier is exact.\n\n**Steps**\n\n- **Baseline recovery rate:** start from `_base_rec`, the recovery_method_factor point value (0.30 in the baseline scenario; 0.70 if you pick the mandatory-deconstruction scenario in the sidebar).\n- **Stack the toggled levers** onto the rate, in list order. The one "set" lever raises the rate to a floor: `_rec = max(_rec, 0.70)`. The four "uplift" levers add: `_rec = _rec + 0.10 / 0.06 / 0.04 / 0.06`. The set lever runs first, so the uplifts stack on top of the 0.70 floor rather than being erased by it.\n- **Cap:** `_rec = min(_rec, 0.86)`, the documented Vancouver full-deconstruction ceiling.\n- **Multiplier:** `_mult = _rec / _base_rec`.\n- **Rescale supply and value:** `after_spec = baseline_spec * _mult` and `after_value = baseline_value * _mult`, where the baselines are the sums of spec_ready_bf and value_cad across the 25 metros. The percent change shown on each metric is `(_mult - 1) * 100`.\n- **Climate benefit** is computed from the after-policy board feet: `co2(bf) = avoided_production + avoided_landfill + biogenic_stored`, where\n  - avoided_production = bf times 0.0023597 times 62.0 divided by 1000\n  - biogenic_stored = bf times 0.0023597 times 785.0 divided by 1000\n  - avoided_landfill = (bf times 0.0023597 times 492.0 divided by 1000) times 217.0 divided by 1000\n  The delta shown is co2(after) minus co2(baseline).\n\n**Worked example (all five levers on, baseline 0.30):** 0.30, then max to 0.70, plus 0.10, plus 0.06, plus 0.04, plus 0.06 gives 0.96, capped to 0.86. Multiplier 0.86 divided by 0.30 is about 2.87, so roughly +187 percent to supply, value and carbon.\n\n**Constants and sources**\n\n- **Mandatory deconstruction bylaw:** sets recovery to 0.70. City of Vancouver Green Demolition Bylaw, about 86 percent recovery for full deconstruction (2014, amended 2019).\n- **Clean-wood landfill ban:** plus 0.10. Metro Vancouver Clean Wood Disposal Ban (2018).\n- **Higher construction-waste tipping fees:** plus 0.06. EREF municipal-waste tipping-fee study; UK Landfill Tax response, WRAP (2023 to 2024).\n- **Public procurement for reclaimed content:** plus 0.04. California CALGreen; CalRecycle model deconstruction ordinance (2022).\n- **Structural re-grading standard:** plus 0.06. SWST evaluation of salvaged framing grade-pass rate (2019).\n- **Combined recovery cap:** 0.86, the Vancouver full-deconstruction outcome (hard-coded in the app).\n- **Baseline recovery_method_factor:** 0.30 (range 0.15 to 0.50). Oregon DEQ 2019; Delta Institute. Blended mixed practice; deconstruction about 0.55, mechanical demolition about 0.05.\n- **Carbon factors:** 0.0023597 m3 per board foot; 62.0 kg CO2e per m3 avoided manufacturing (Athena 2018; Canadian HWP review 2024); 785.0 kg CO2e per m3 biogenic carbon stored (Athena 2018); 492.0 kg per m3 wood density; 217.0 kg CO2e per tonne landfill methane avoided (Napier 2007 via US EPA WARM and Athena, in USDA FS Bergman et al. 2013).\n\nNote: modelling every policy through one recovery rate is a deliberate simplification, stated in the app. The lever values are sourced but represent single-policy response estimates, and the 0.86 cap keeps stacked policies from exceeding the best documented real-world recovery outcome.',
    'Economics': "This page is a sourced snapshot of the reclaimed-lumber economics. Every number here is a fixed, cited constant read straight from `config/demand.py`. The page does no modelling of its own and does not reuse the supply cascade, so nothing here is forecast or estimated by the app.\n\n**How each figure is built**\n\n- **Reclaimed price premium (20 to 50%):** reads `reclaimed_premium` = (0.20, 0.35, 0.50) and shows `int(low times 100)` to `int(high times 100)`, so 20% to 50% over virgin lumber. The middle value 0.35 is stored but not shown.\n- **Deconstruction cost premium (17 to 25%):** reads `deconstruction_premium` = (0.17, 0.21, 0.25) and shows `int(low times 100)` to `int(high times 100)`, so 17% to 25% more than mechanical demolition. The middle value 0.21 is not shown.\n- **Metro Vancouver salvage value ($342M/yr, 2020):** a fixed label. It matches `metro_van_salvage_value` = 342 times 1,000,000 CAD per year.\n- **Deconstruction versus demolition cost bars:** each bar starts at the low cost and its length is `high minus low`. Mechanical demolition is $4 to $10 per sq ft and takes 1 to 4 days; full deconstruction is $8 to $16 per sq ft and takes 2 or more weeks (US dollars, indicative ranges).\n- **Net cost after wood-donation tax credits (CAD per house):** a waterfall that starts at gross deconstruction of $43,000, subtracts a federal donation credit of $18,850, subtracts a provincial donation credit of $9,555, and lands at a net of $14,595 (43,000 minus 18,850 minus 9,555 = 14,595). A dashed line marks the demolition baseline of $26,500, so net deconstruction sits below demolition. This is an illustrative single-firm case; the credit depends on appraisal and the donor's tax position.\n- **Jobs per project:** fixed values, 6 for full deconstruction versus 1 for mechanical demolition.\n- **Bottleneck severity:** each ranked bottleneck is plotted at `severity = 6 minus rank`, so rank 1 (structural-reuse code wall) is severity 5, the most binding, and rank 5 is severity 1. This ranking is the project's own read of severity, not an external source.\n\n**Constants and sources**\n\n- reclaimed_premium: 0.20 / 0.35 / 0.50 fraction over virgin. Source: Light House SME report, March 2026.\n- deconstruction_premium: 0.17 / 0.21 / 0.25 fraction over demolition. Source: Light House / Delta Institute.\n- metro_van_salvage_value: 342,000,000 CAD per year, Metro Vancouver. Source: Vancouver Economic Commission, Unbuilders and BCIT, Business Case for Deconstruction, July 2020.\n- Deconstruction vs demolition costs: mechanical $4 to $10, deconstruction $8 to $16 per sq ft. Source: Angi (cost, 2026); DemoPros (2025) and Journal of Commerce (2021) for time.\n- Net cost case: gross $43,000, federal credit minus $18,850, provincial credit minus $9,555, net $14,595; demolition baseline $26,500 (all CAD per house). Source: Unbuilders / Habitat via The Construction Source (2023) and CBC (2020).\n- Jobs per project: 1 (demolition) versus 6 (deconstruction). Source: Journal of Commerce / ConstructConnect (2021).\n- Bottleneck severity: 6 minus rank. This is the project's own severity judgement, not a cited figure.\n\nNote on value per board foot: the dollar figures behind the premium are not shown on this page, but the app's assumptions register reclaimed lumber at 5.00 (low), 6.50 (medium) and 9.00 (high) CAD per board foot against a virgin reference of 4.45 CAD per board foot (dealer price lists 2023 to 2026 and NAHB, at USD to CAD 1.37). Medium reclaimed over virgin is about 1.46, roughly a 46% premium, which is consistent with the 20 to 50% band displayed.",
    'Demand segments': '**How this is calculated.** The two demand totals on this page come straight from the segment table in `config/demand.py`. Each of the 15 segments carries a low, point, and high volume in millions of board feet per year, plus a tier label. Tier A is demand that is legal today (non-structural, aesthetic uses); Tier B is demand that only opens up if the building code allows structural reuse. The totals are plain sums, with no weighting.\n\nSteps:\n- **Convert to board feet:** every segment\'s low, point, and high figure is multiplied by M (1,000,000) to turn "M bf/yr" into raw board feet.\n- **Tier A total (legal today):** add up the point value of every Tier A segment. That is (3.5 + 3.5 + 2.75 + 2.5 + 2.5 + 2.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 0.75 + 0.75 + 0.4) times M = **22.15M bf/yr**.\n- **Tier A band:** add all the Tier A lows together and all the Tier A highs together, giving **17.80M to 26.50M bf/yr**. This adds the ranges as if they move together, so it is the widest band, not a statistical blend.\n- **Tier B total (needs a code change):** add up the point value of every Tier B segment. That is (45 + 18 + 15 + 10) times M = **88.00M bf/yr**, with a band of **55.00M to 135.00M bf/yr** built the same way.\n- **No weighting:** each segment counts at its full value. The only filter is the tier label, and the only math is the sum plus the M multiplier.\n- **Supply comparison:** the "spec-ready supply" figure shown next to these totals is not part of the demand sum. It comes from the supply model (spec-ready board feet summed across all metros) and is displayed so you can see that latent demand sits well above current supply.\n\nConstants and sources:\n- **M = 1,000,000** board feet per "M bf" (config/demand.py).\n- **Per-segment volumes** (low / point / high, M bf/yr): these are the project\'s bottom-up estimates, not figures pulled from a single report. The segment categories follow the ECCC construction, renovation, and demolition wood-demand taxonomy, and the estimates are calibrated to the market\'s structure (flooring and furniture lead demand).\n- **Tier A segments:** Hospitality & restaurant fit-out 3.0/3.5/4.0; Residential feature & renovation 3.0/3.5/4.0; Retail fit-out & display 2.5/2.75/3.0; Office / commercial tenant improvement 2.0/2.5/3.0; Furniture & millwork 2.0/2.5/3.0; Flooring 2.0/2.5/3.0; Cladding & siding 1.0/1.5/2.0; Landscaping & public realm 1.0/1.5/2.0; Film, TV & events 0.5/0.75/1.0; Acoustic & architectural panels 0.5/0.75/1.0; Crafts & artisan 0.3/0.4/0.5.\n- **Tier B segments (code-gated):** Residential structural framing reuse 30/45/70; Mass timber / NLT / DLT feedstock 10/18/30; Industrial / dunnage / non-spec structural 10/15/20; Modular / panelized / ADU 5/10/15.\n- **Context anchors** (sanity check only, different units from board feet): North American reclaimed-lumber market about US$12.4 billion (2024, Global Growth Insights / market.us); Canadian value-retention economy about C$56 billion (2019, Constructive Voices / TheFutureEconomy.ca).\n- **Page sources:** ECCC circularity of wood in construction workshop report (Feb 2024); Habitat for Humanity Canada ReStore (2024).\n\nOne honest caveat: the board-foot numbers per segment are estimates given deliberately as ranges, so treat the point totals as the middle of a wide band rather than a measured value. The Tier B band in particular is large because structural reuse is not yet legal, so that demand is latent.',
    'Demand drivers': "**How this is calculated.** This chart projects legal-today (Tier A) reclaimed-wood demand forward from a 2026 base and compares it to the national spec-ready supply forecast. Tier A grows at the reclaimed-lumber market's own rate. An optional scenario adds structural reuse (Tier B) once a building-code path opens in 2030.\n\n- **Base demand (2026):** base Tier A demand is the sum of every legal-today segment's point estimate = 22.15M bf/yr. Base Tier B (structural reuse) is the sum of the code-gated segments = 88M bf/yr.\n- **Grow Tier A each year** (year h = 0 to 10, so 2026 to 2036): central = base Tier A times (1 + 0.045) to the power h. The band runs low = base Tier A times (1 + 0.03)^h up to high = base Tier A times (1 + 0.07)^h.\n- **Optional 2030 code-path step (Tier B):** if the scenario is on, then from 2030 a phase-in ramp = min(1, (year minus 2030 plus 1) divided by 3) reaches full over three years (one third in 2030, two thirds in 2031, full from 2032). The added volume = base Tier B times ramp, laid on top: central plus add, high plus add, low plus add times 0.6.\n- **Compare to supply:** the green line is the national spec-ready supply forecast summed by year. Both series are drawn in millions of board feet.\n- **Headline numbers:** Demand 2026 = 22.15M bf. Demand 2036 central = 22.15M times 1.045^10 = about 34.40M bf. Supply 2036 central comes from the supply forecast. With the code path on, Demand 2036 central steps to about 122.40M bf.\n\n**Constants and sources**\n- **Tier A growth band:** low 3%/yr, central 4.5%/yr, high 7%/yr per year (DEMAND_GROWTH). Sourced to reclaimed-lumber market growth, about 4 to 5% central with a 3 to 7% band (market.us; Precedence Research, 2024-2025).\n- **Green-building pull rate:** 10.6%/yr (GREEN_PULL_RATE, Mordor Intelligence, 2025). Shown for context as the demand-pull ceiling; it is not applied to the growth math, which tops out at 7%.\n- **Base year 2026, horizon 10 years** (config/assumptions.py FORECAST: anchor year and a 5 to 10 year brief horizon).\n- **Code-path year 2030:** a chosen what-if in the checkbox, not a sourced date.\n- **Tier B phase-in 3 years, and low-band Tier B counts at 60%:** both are modeling choices, not sourced.\n- **Base Tier A 22.15M bf/yr and base Tier B 88M bf/yr:** bottom-up segment point estimates (config/demand.py DEMAND_SEGMENTS), given as ranges on purpose and calibrated to the roughly US$12.4B North American reclaimed-lumber market (2024-2025), so treat them as estimates, not observed volumes.\n\nThis is a growth-rate projection from sourced market rates, not a bottom-up yearly demand model, and the band reflects the growth range rather than a full Monte Carlo.",
}
